Run no BIOS syscall handler for a Linux syscall, not just the two that write.

PR SCOPE: IN. Part of the minimal set needed to run Linux -- TLB/MMU/syscall
emulation correctness. Belongs with ee-tlb-fixes.patch.

Requires: ee-tlb-fixes.patch

That patch guards GetOsdConfigParam and GetOsdConfigParam2 on v0 holding a Linux
syscall number, because those two write to the address in a0. Three more
handlers READ guest memory at an address taken from a0/a1, which under Linux are
ordinary syscall arguments:

    SetOsdConfigParam2      memRead8(memaddr++)
    SetVTLBRefillHandler    memRead32(addr) x4
    the console-log call    memRead32(a1), then PSM() on the result

Those reads go through the vtlb, so a bad address raises a TLB miss charged to
the syscall instruction itself. The guest kernel then kills the process with
SIGSEGV at a pc that performs no memory access:

    do_page_fault() #2: sending SIGSEGV to rcS for illegal read access from
    00000000 (epc == 2ac4e43c ...)   insn @ epc: 24020fa3 0000000c 14e0fff7
                                                 li v0,4003; syscall; bne

SetGsCrt is unguarded too and yanks the video mode whenever v1 happens to select
it, which is where the constant "Mode %x is not supported. Report me upstream"
came from.

The original comment notes that skipping the whole switch broke the SBIOS, which
faulted in sbcall_cdvdinit -- v0 alone is not a reliable discriminator. Add
where the syscall came from: TGE's SBIOS runs from KSEG0/KSEG1 (0x8xxxxxxx /
0xAxxxxxxx), Linux userspace lives below 0x80000000, and the Linux kernel never
executes a syscall instruction itself. Requiring both makes the test safe to
apply to the whole switch, and sbcall_cdvdinit is unaffected.

Tested: BlackRhino Linux init, which previously lost rcS and grep to SIGSEGV
during boot, now reaches runlevel 2 and an X session. NOT tested against games,
though by construction the guard cannot fire for one: a BIOS call does not put
4000-4999 in v0, and PS2 titles do not execute syscalls from user-segment
addresses.

--- a/pcsx2/R5900OpcodeImpl.cpp
+++ b/pcsx2/R5900OpcodeImpl.cpp
@@ -942,8 +942,15 @@
 	// on it. Skipping the whole switch breaks the SBIOS, which needs some of
 	// these; it faulted in sbcall_cdvdinit. Mode is not a discriminator either:
 	// PS2 threads run in user mode, which is why the syscall interface exists.
+	// v0 alone is not enough to tell the callers apart, which is why an earlier
+	// version of this guard could only be applied to the two handlers that write
+	// memory. Add where the syscall came FROM: TGE's SBIOS runs from KSEG0/KSEG1
+	// (0x8xxxxxxx / 0xAxxxxxxx), while Linux userspace lives below 0x80000000 and
+	// the Linux kernel never executes a syscall instruction itself. Requiring
+	// both makes the test safe to apply to the whole switch.
 	const bool linuxSyscall =
-		cpuRegs.GPR.n.v0.UL[0] >= 4000 && cpuRegs.GPR.n.v0.UL[0] < 5000;
+		cpuRegs.GPR.n.v0.UL[0] >= 4000 && cpuRegs.GPR.n.v0.UL[0] < 5000 &&
+		cpuRegs.pc < 0x80000000;
 
 	if (cpuRegs.GPR.n.v1.SL[0] < 0)
 		call = (u8)(-cpuRegs.GPR.n.v1.SL[0]);
@@ -952,6 +959,16 @@
 
 	BIOS_LOG("Bios call: %s (%x)", R5900::bios[call], call);
 
+	if (linuxSyscall)
+	{
+		// Run no handler at all: deliver the exception to the guest kernel and
+		// nothing else. Falling through the switch is not enough, because
+		// several handlers touch guest memory before any case is rejected.
+		cpuRegs.pc -= 4;
+		cpuException(0x20, cpuRegs.branch);
+		return;
+	}
+
 
 	switch (static_cast<Syscall>(call))
 	{
